\title{13 - Workload characterization: what the kernel does on Cedar-level corpora}
\taxon{Study}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/13-workload-characterization.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Question (owner, on the static heat spike of \code{\verb~|spikes/machine~}): "You need to check Cedar level workload. Init may not be interesting enough." How hot do the code objects of the machine (\code{\verb~|TELE~}, \code{\verb~|BODY~}, \code{\verb~|RULE~}, ISA §3.2) get on realistic corpora, and which specialisations does the data argue for?}

\p{Evidence tags: \strong{\verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~} measured here (tools and raw data in section 8), \strong{\verb~|[~est\verb~|]~} my estimate with the arithmetic shown, \strong{\verb~|[~src\verb~|]~} read in a checker's source. Numbers without a tag are measured. Instruction counts are user-mode instructions on this aarch64 host (cedar: 71 per byte here, 70 published for x86). Corpora: \code{\verb~|work/exports/{init,std,cedar,reachability,con-leche,navier-stokes-euler,cslib,mathlib}.ndjson~}.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Decision summary}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Cedar is not more reduction-heavy than Init; it is less.} Share of nano-lean's checking instructions spent under a reduction (whnf, delta, iota, projection, including the evaluation and interning it triggers): Init 38\%, cedar's own 22,454 declarations 17\%, NSE's own 17\%, cslib's own 24\%, con-leche's own 34\%, reachability's own 99\%. What distinguishes Cedar-level declarations is \strong{size and conversion}: 1.02 M instructions per declaration against 0.38 M for Init, 30\% of their instructions under \code{\verb~|def_eq~} against 16\%. Real kernel computation exists in exactly three places: reachability (5 \code{\verb~|decide~} proofs = 92.5\% of the file), con-leche (one 5.8 G declaration, 20 reduction-heavy proofs in its top 50) and the arena's \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests.}
\li{\strong{The corpora contain their libraries.} By instructions cedar's export is 39.5\% Init, 14.8\% Std, 5.9\% Batteries/Mathlib and 39.8\% Cedar; cslib is 70\% Mathlib and 5.4\% Cslib; NSE is 72\% project, con-leche 94\%, reachability 93\% (section 3.2). A per-corpus number is mostly a statement about Lean's core library.}
\li{\strong{Dynamic heat is low in absolute terms and never reaches the promotion rent.} Whole cedar (64 G instructions): 4.15 M delta unfoldings, 1.24 M iota steps, 1.06 M projections of constructors, 7.5 M application inferences. No \code{\verb~|BODY~}, \code{\verb~|RULE~} or projection object is executed 10^6 times in any corpus, mathlib included (hottest: \code{\verb~|PProd.mk.0~} 723 k, \code{\verb~|DFunLike.coe~} 685 k, \code{\verb~|List.rec~} cons rule 582 k); one \code{\verb~|TELE~} object (\code{\verb~|Eq~}: 3.1 M on mathlib, 1.4 M on cslib) is. Objects above 10^5 executions: cedar 7 bodies, 3 rules, 3 projections, 10 telescopes; mathlib 40, 11, 17, 61 (section 3.4). At 3-4 k instructions per reduction step \verb~|[~est\verb~|]~ the ISA's rent of 3.3 G needs about 10^6 executions of one block: \strong{no corpus reaches it; two \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests do} (\code{\verb~|unfold-before-literal-fallback~}, \code{\verb~|proj-applied-struct-arg-bad~}: 3.15 M \code{\verb~|Nat.rec~} steps and 1.57 M \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}/\code{\verb~|Add.add~} unfoldings each).}
\li{\strong{The hot set is Lean's core library, and it is the same everywhere.} 93.6\% of cedar's delta unfoldings and 94.9\% of its iota steps are of constants declared in Init; Cedar's own definitions receive 2.9\% (con-leche's: 6.5\%). The 300 bodies that are hottest on Init cover 86\% of cedar's unfoldings, 82\% of con-leche's, 74\% of reachability's, but only 31-51\% on the Mathlib-based corpora, whose heat is in Mathlib's structure projections (\code{\verb~|DFunLike.coe~}, \code{\verb~|AddCommMonoid.toAddMonoid~}, \code{\verb~|CategoryTheory.Functor.obj~}). By role (cedar): \code{\verb~|casesOn~} 21.5\%, structure projection functions 19.8\%, matchers 14.2\%, \code{\verb~|brecOn~}/\code{\verb~|below~} 6.4\%, compiler auxiliaries (\code{\verb~|_f~}, \code{\verb~|.go~}) 8.8\%, other definitions 26.5\%.}
\li{\strong{Derived reduction rules (nano-lean's, the model of K2) are the one specialisation with a measured large effect, and only on evaluation}: switching them off costs 1.9x on reachability, 10.3x on \code{\verb~|magma-list-deep-n36~}, 3.2x on \code{\verb~|ite-taken-branch-bad~}, 1.6x on \code{\verb~|magma-list-pair-n21~}, but 1.04-1.06x on init, std, cedar, con-leche and 1.00-1.02x on NSE, cslib, mathlib.}
\li{\strong{Where instructions go on Cedar-level work} (whole cedar, nano-lean): inference 42\%, reduction 25\%, conversion 20\%, parsing 9\%. By what the code is doing: walking terms and environments (\code{\verb~|eval~}/\code{\verb~|apply~}/\code{\verb~|trim~}) 33\%, building and interning values 21\% (+4\% hash tables), inference proper 18\%, conversion proper 7\%; on the evaluation tests interning is 33-49\%. Hash-consing is the part no code tier removes.}
}

\p{Conclusions for the machine (details in section 5):}

\ul{
\li{(i) \code{\verb~|BODY~}/\code{\verb~|RULE~} heat is far more concentrated than \code{\verb~|TELE~} heat (cedar: 100 bodies = 74\%, 10 rules = 85\%, 100 telescopes = 62\%; Init static telescopes: 100 = 72\%) but 10-100 times smaller in volume than the rent assumes. Objects at >= 10^4 / 10^5 / 10^6 / 10^7 executions (bodies + rules + projections + telescopes): cedar 175 / 23 / 0 / 0, reachability 39 / 10 / 0 / 0, con-leche 176 / 22 / 0 / 0, NSE 307 / 18 / 0 / 0, cslib 463 / 42 / 1 / 0, mathlib 1073 / 129 / 1 / 0.}
\li{(ii) Compiled (Truffle) code has no target on init, std, cedar, NSE, cslib, mathlib. Its only candidates are closed evaluation loops: the \code{\verb~|perf/~} evaluation tests, reachability's \code{\verb~|decide~} proofs, a handful of con-leche proofs. Elaborated code (K1) for \code{\verb~|TELE~} and for the Init hot set pays everywhere because a third of all instructions is term walking; K2 pays on evaluation only. 57\% of cedar's declarations perform no reduction step at all and 81\% at most ten: for them the generic walk (K0) and the memo tables are the whole machine.}
\li{(iii) Argued for by frequency: instance-projection shortcuts (structure projection function + projection of a constructor: 20\% of unfoldings on cedar, 37-49\% on Mathlib corpora), \code{\verb~|casesOn~}/matcher decision trees (36\% on cedar, 68\% on reachability), derived rules for structural recursion (\code{\verb~|brecOn~}: 6\% of unfoldings, and \code{\verb~|PProd.mk.0~} alone is 28\% of all projections). Argued for only by the \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests: Nat immediates (94-99.8\% of Nat literal operations fit 31 bits, but a whole corpus performs 54 k-481 k of them). Not argued for by any corpus: packed \code{\verb~|String~}/\code{\verb~|Array~}/\code{\verb~|BitVec~} (at most 4.3 k string literal expansions per corpus, longest 19 characters), packed \code{\verb~|List~} (lists are built by evaluation, not given as literals).}
\li{(iv) Fast benchmarks (section 6): \code{\verb~|work/workloads/slices/cedar-hot-ax.ndjson~} (133 MB, 13.6 G, 3.4 s in nano-lean: Cedar's 172 most expensive own declarations over a statements-only library; inference 47\%, conversion 35\%, reduction 13\%) for Cedar-level work, \code{\verb~|reachability.ndjson~} (19 MB, 11 G, 3.4 s) for evaluation, \code{\verb~|perf/unfold-before-literal-bad~} (5.3 G, 184 MiB of native stack) for depth and Nat literals.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. Method, and what the numbers are}

\table{
\tr{\th{Source}\th{What it gives}\th{How}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|bench/workloads/static-shape.rs~} (ours)}\td{static shape; reproduces the spike's HEAT lines exactly on init}\td{one pass over the NDJSON, host}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean v0.4.2, instrumented copy (\code{\verb~|work/workloads/build/nano-lean-instrumented~})}\td{per-declaration instructions; per-constant delta, iota, projection, telescope counts; Nat/String literal events; whnf chains; stack depth; phase split}\td{Docker; three builds: plain + per-declaration \code{\verb~|perf_event~} counter (\code{\verb~|di~}), \code{\verb~|vstats~} (nano-lean's own counters + ours), frame pointers (\code{\verb~|perf record~})}}
\tr{\td{rpylean \code{\verb~|check --stats --print jsonl~} (unmodified binary)}\td{delta per constant, iota per recursor, native Nat ops, def_eq totals, time per declaration}\td{leader's own facility}}
\tr{\td{gruszka \code{\verb~|--features knobs~}, \code{\verb~|SK_DECL_INSTR=1~} (unmodified source, rebuilt)}\td{ticks, whnf misses, unfolds, def_eq, infer misses per declaration; ground-machine runs}\td{leader's own facility}}
}

\p{Why nano-lean: arena \verb~|#~1, the architecture RLVM follows (folded constants, memo on values, hash-consed closures, derived rules), per-declaration counters already there, an 80 s rebuild. The patch adds counters only (\code{\verb~|src/wl.rs~}, \code{\verb~|wl!~} sites); the \code{\verb~|di~} build has identical checking code. The leaders' costs on the \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests are read from the baseline team's \code{\verb~|work/baselines/small/*.json~}.}

\p{Definitions (all counted where nano-lean's memo misses, i.e. real executions). \strong{BODY} = a definition's body is applied to a spine (delta), from \code{\verb~|whnf~} or from lazy delta; with derived rules on, a \strong{rule firing} replaces the unfolding and the iota steps under it. \strong{RULE} = a recursor rule is applied, keyed \code{\verb~|(recursor, rule index)~}. \strong{PROJ} = projection of a constructor application, keyed \code{\verb~|(constructor, field)~}. \strong{TELE} = an application spine with a constant head is inferred, or a neutral value with a constant head is typed. \strong{Phase} of an instruction sample = the innermost enclosing function among inference, conversion, reduction, import (frame-pointer call graphs, one sample per 2,000,003 user instructions; \code{\verb~|eval~}, \code{\verb~|apply~} and interning helpers are charged to the phase that called them).}

\p{Caveats. (a) One checking thread and no PGO: totals are 10\% above the arena build (mathlib 399 G against the baseline team's 361 G). (b) Counts are nano-lean's: its memo tables, budgeted argument probes and declaration order decide how often a body runs; rpylean (substitution kernel) and gruszka (NbE) agree on the order of magnitude (section 3.7), the official kernel was not instrumented. (c) Wall times are from a loaded host. (d) "Origin" of a name is the first of the init, std, mathlib exports that declares it; everything else is "project" (for cedar, "Mathlib" means Batteries and the part of Mathlib it imports).}
}

