\p{Chains and depth. A non-memoised \code{\verb~|whnf~} call takes one iteration (a \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} and at most one delta or Nat step) in 46\% of the cases on cedar, 2-7 in 51\%, 8-63 in 3.3\%, 64 or more in 205 calls; the longest chain is 194 on cedar, 325 on con-leche, 307 on reachability (1,498 with derived rules off), 630 on mathlib (3,149 off). The deepest native recursion of nano-lean in any corpus declaration is 952 KiB of stack (mathlib, \code{\verb~|Real.sin_pos_of_pos_of_le_one._proof_1_3~}; 937 KiB on reachability): no corpus declaration needs 1 MiB, the \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests need 184 MiB (section 4). Closed terms: 56\% of cedar's delta steps and 68\% of its \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} steps have no free variable in the subject (reachability 84\% / 99\%, NSE 29\% / 34\%, mathlib 22\% / 35\%).}

\p{Closed-term engines of the leaders. gruszka's ground machine (call-by-need, entered when a declaration's arena passes 2^26 values) ran \strong{0 times on all eight corpora} (its own counter \code{\verb~|gr~}, knobs build). rpylean's CEK machine has no counter. Its \code{\verb~|--stats~} reports 25 k delta unfoldings on reachability against 1.69 M iota and 31 M beta steps (nano-lean: 3.42 M delta with rules off, gruszka 2.29 M), consistent with the six \code{\verb~|Bench.Arena.*._proof_1~} theorems (\code{\verb~|Decidable.decide p = true~}) running inside the machine or in untraced fast paths \verb~|[~inferred; src: \code{\verb~|eval_closed_scalar~} is called only when a closed term meets a \code{\verb~|Bool~}/\code{\verb~|Nat~} scalar\verb~|]~.}
}

