\p{Hot objects: \code{\verb~|unfold-before-literal-*~} and \code{\verb~|proj-applied-struct-*~} run one loop per theorem, 2^19 iterations and 2^19 deep: per iteration two \code{\verb~|Nat.rec~} steps on a literal (nano-lean builds a \code{\verb~|Nat.succ~} cell each time), two rule firings (\code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}, \code{\verb~|Add.add~}), two projections and one native add; derived rules make it slower (0.88). \code{\verb~|magma-list-pair-n21~}: \code{\verb~|Nat.decidableBallLT._f~}, \code{\verb~|Bool.casesOn~}, \code{\verb~|Decidable.decide~}, \code{\verb~|Nat.decEq~}, \code{\verb~|instDecidableEqFin~} at 194-204k each. \code{\verb~|magma-list-deep-n36~}: \code{\verb~|List.get!Internal._f~} 841k (list indexing by structural recursion; with derived rules off 3.44 M unfoldings and 1.70 M \code{\verb~|List.rec~} steps). \code{\verb~|magma-string-pair-n9~}: one 179-character literal expanded once, then \code{\verb~|List.concat._f~} 31k, \code{\verb~|List.get._f~} 16k. These are the only inputs on which code objects exceed 10^6 executions (2 bodies and 2 rules in the first two tests; 12 bodies and 3 rules above 10^5 in \code{\verb~|magma-list-pair-n21~}), and on which the leaders differ by 10-100x; they are decided by algorithmic choices (literal folding order, derived rules, a call-by-need machine without interning: rpylean is best on both magma-list tests) before any code quality matters.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Conclusions for the machine}

\p{\strong{(i) Heat and promotion thresholds.} Counts per corpus are in section 3.4. Translating executions into the ISA's rent: reduction-phase instructions per reduction step are 16.0 G / 5.4 M = 3.0 k on cedar and 10.3 G / 2.6 M = 4.0 k on reachability \verb~|[~est: phase share x process instructions / (delta + firings + iota + proj); nano-lean's cost, including the evaluation and interning under the step\verb~|]~. A block that owns 3 k instructions per execution reaches RENT = 3.3 G after 1.1 M executions. Nothing in a corpus does; the hottest body of mathlib (\code{\verb~|DFunLike.coe~}, 685 k) has consumed at most about 2 G by the end of the run. A threshold that would let T2 see anything on cedar has to sit at 10^4-10^5 executions (62 / 7 bodies, 11 / 3 rules), where the remaining work is 51\% / 8\% of unfoldings, i.e. at most 25\% x 0.51 = 13\% (10^4) or 2\% (10^5) of cedar's instructions \verb~|[~est: reduction share x share after crossing\verb~|]~, of which compiled code removes only the walking part (11.6 / 25.0). An upper bound for T2 on cedar is therefore about 6\% at a 10^4 threshold, before compilation cost (0.4-0.5 G per root x 73 roots = 29-37 G, half of cedar's total) \verb~|[~est\verb~|]~. \strong{T2 cannot pay on library corpora under any threshold.} ISA §8.5 expects the hottest telescopes and projection bodies of mathlib to run 10^6-10^7 times; measured with one worker: \code{\verb~|Eq~} 3.1 M, the next telescope 0.92 M, the hottest projection body 0.69 M, the hottest rule 0.58 M - and a quarter of that per worker with four workers.}

\p{\strong{(ii) Which tier for which workload.}}

\table{
\tr{\th{workload}\th{where}\th{K0 generic walk}\th{K1 elaborated code}\th{K2 derived rules}\th{T2 compiled}}
\tr{\td{large inference/conversion proofs}\td{all library corpora; 57-81\% of declarations do <= 10 reductions}\td{carries 60-75\% of instructions (inference + conversion phases)}\td{\code{\verb~|TELE~} of the top 100-1000 constants (62-85\% of 7.5 M executions on cedar); \code{\verb~|BODY~} of the Init hot set}\td{1.00-1.06x}\td{nothing to compile}}
\tr{\td{reduction-heavy proofs inside large terms (\code{\verb~|omega~}/\code{\verb~|grind~} certificates, con-leche \code{\verb~|keyOf_*~}, mathlib \code{\verb~|decide~})}\td{3-20 of a corpus's top 50; 5-18\% of instructions}\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|BODY~}/\code{\verb~|RULE~} of 100-300 Init objects}\td{1.1-1.5x per declaration, 2.5x at most (measured \code{\verb~|nod x~})}\td{no: 10^4-10^5 steps per declaration}}
\tr{\td{closed evaluation (reachability's 5 proofs, \code{\verb~|magma-*~}, \code{\verb~|unfold-before-literal*~}, \code{\verb~|proj-applied-struct*~}, \code{\verb~|ite-taken-branch-bad~})}\td{1 corpus + about 15 \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests}\td{}\td{all of the work}\td{1.6-10x (0.88x on the literal loops)}\td{the only candidate: 2-17 blocks at >= 10^5-10^6 executions; bounded by interning (33-49\% of instructions)}}
\tr{\td{big-number arithmetic (\code{\verb~|gf3-matrix-product~})}\td{1 test}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{no: 86\% in the bignum library, 24\% hashing literals}}
}

\p{For the first row the levers are the ones the leaders already use and RLVM's design already has: memo hit rates (infer 67\%, eval 47\%, def_eq 84\%), not evaluating unread arguments, cheap interning. One third of all instructions is term walking in every corpus, which is the argument for elaborating \strong{telescopes} (12.9\% of cedar's instructions are evaluation under inference) at least as much as bodies.}

\p{\strong{(iii) Specialisations, with the measured frequency of each pattern.}}

\table{
\tr{\th{specialisation}\th{pattern frequency}\th{verdict}}
\tr{\td{instance-projection shortcut (a structure projection function applied to a constructor: \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd α α α inst a b~} -> field)}\td{structure projection functions are 19.8\% of unfoldings on cedar, 49.0\% on NSE, 42.8\% on mathlib; projections of constructors 1.06 M on cedar, 9.6 M on mathlib; \code{\verb~|HAdd.mk.0~} + \code{\verb~|Add.mk.0~} = 22\% of cedar's}\td{do it in K1 (the ISA's \code{\verb~|PROJARG~} LEAF shape): the most frequent reduction pattern of every Mathlib-based corpus}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|casesOn~} / matcher decision trees (case of known constructor without building motive and minors)}\td{\code{\verb~|casesOn~} + matchers = 35.7\% of unfoldings on cedar, 67.5\% on reachability, 51.4\% on con-leche, 15\% on mathlib; each is followed by an iota step (1.24 M on cedar)}\td{do it (K2 \code{\verb~|RULES~}): this is what nano-lean's rules buy, 1.9x on reachability}}
\tr{\td{derived rules for structural recursion (\code{\verb~|brecOn~}/\code{\verb~|below~}, \code{\verb~|PProd~} tables, \code{\verb~|_f~} auxiliaries)}\td{\code{\verb~|brecOn~}/\code{\verb~|below~} 6.4\% + auxiliaries 8.8\% of cedar's unfoldings; \code{\verb~|PProd.mk.0~} = 28.2\% of cedar's projections, 91.6\% of reachability's}\td{do it together with the previous line; removes most \code{\verb~|PProd~} traffic}}
\tr{\td{packed mutual/well-founded arguments (\code{\verb~|PSum~}, \code{\verb~|PSigma~}, \code{\verb~|WellFounded.fix~})}\td{con-leche: \code{\verb~|PSum.casesOn~} 314 k unfoldings, \code{\verb~|PSum.rec~} 303 k iota, \code{\verb~|PSum.inr~} 824 k telescope executions (one declaration is 10\% of the corpus); \code{\verb~|WellFounded.fix~}/\code{\verb~|Acc.rec~}: 451 static spines on cedar}\td{cover \code{\verb~|PSum~}/\code{\verb~|PSigma~} \code{\verb~|casesOn~} by the decision-tree rule; nothing special for \code{\verb~|WellFounded.fix~}}}
\tr{\td{Nat small-integer fast path (owner note 7)}\td{94.4-99.8\% of Nat literal operations have operands <= 31 bits, but a corpus executes only 54 k-481 k of them (cedar: 117 k in 64 G instructions) and 17 k-278 k iota steps on literals; \code{\verb~|perf/~}: 1.57 M adds + 3.15 M literal iota in one test, 197 k \code{\verb~|beq~} in another}\td{cheap and right, but invisible on corpora; it is a \code{\verb~|perf/~} feature (literal \code{\verb~|Nat.rec~} without a \code{\verb~|Nat.succ~} cell is the measurable part). Big operands (>= 2^63: 0-3.0 k operations per corpus; 50.8 k on 1.2 Mbit numbers = 86\% of \code{\verb~|gf3-matrix-product~}) need only a good bignum library and cheap hashing of big literals}}
\tr{\td{packed \code{\verb~|String~} / \code{\verb~|Char~}}\td{string literal expansions: 0-4,320 per corpus, at most 19 characters, 13-18 k characters in total on cedar and con-leche; \code{\verb~|magma-string-pair-n9~}: one 179-character literal}\td{not justified}}
\tr{\td{packed \code{\verb~|List~} / \code{\verb~|Array~} / \code{\verb~|BitVec~} storage strategies (owner note 8)}\td{\code{\verb~|List.rec~} is the hottest rule everywhere (cedar 45\% of iota, reachability 56\%) but the lists are produced by evaluation (\code{\verb~|List.map._f~}, \code{\verb~|List.filter~}), not literals; \code{\verb~|Array.rec~} 2.4 k, \code{\verb~|ByteArray.rec~} 3.6 k, \code{\verb~|BitVec.ofFin.0~} 6.8 k on cedar}\td{not justified by any corpus; keep the tag space, build nothing}}
\tr{\td{not interning transient values}\td{construction + interning + hash tables = 25\% of instructions on cedar, 42\% on reachability; construction alone 33-49\% on the evaluation tests; rpylean's CEK machine (no interning) is 2x faster than nano-lean on \code{\verb~|magma-list-pair-n21~} and 1.7x on \code{\verb~|magma-list-deep-n36~}}\td{the largest lever on evaluation workloads, and the one a compiled tier needs (escape analysis cannot remove a hash-cons probe); design question for ISA §10}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Fast benchmarks for iterating on the machine}

\p{\src{bench/workloads/slice-export.py} cuts an export down to the dependency cone of named declarations and renumbers the ids; with \code{\verb~|--axiomatize~}, library theorems are kept as axioms (statement only), so that only the named declarations and the definitions they need are checked. All three slices below are accepted by the official \code{\verb~|leanchecker --from-export~} (4.36.0) and by nano-lean.}

\table{
\tr{\th{benchmark}\th{file}\th{size}\th{decls}\th{nano-lean \code{\verb~|di~}}\th{phases (inference / conversion / reduction / import)}\th{use}}
\tr{\td{Cedar's 172 most expensive own declarations (50\% of Cedar's own cost), statements-only library}\td{\code{\verb~|work/workloads/slices/cedar-hot-ax.ndjson~}}\td{133 MB}\td{9,747}\td{13.6 G, 3.4 s}\td{43.7 / 32.3 / 11.9 / 6.7\%}\td{\strong{the Cedar-level benchmark}: 91.6\% of its checking instructions are in the 172 proofs; official checker 20 s}}
\tr{\td{the same with the full cone (library proofs checked)}\td{\code{\verb~|slices/cedar-hot.ndjson~}}\td{294 MB}\td{24,176}\td{25.0 G, 7.4 s}\td{44.1 / 26.9 / 17.9 / 7.6\%}\td{arena-faithful variant (no added axioms); 50\% in the 172 proofs}}
\tr{\td{every 16th of Cedar's own declarations by cost rank, full cone}\td{\code{\verb~|slices/cedar-s16.ndjson~}}\td{337 MB}\td{30,301}\td{26.6 G, 8.5 s}\td{43.3 / 25.4 / 20.0 / 8.2\%}\td{unbiased sample of Cedar proofs (1,403 targets = 1.5 G), dominated by its library cone}}
\tr{\td{evaluation}\td{\code{\verb~|work/exports/reachability.ndjson~}}\td{19 MB}\td{4,846}\td{11.1 G, 3.4 s}\td{4.1 / 1.2 / 93.0 / 1.2\%}\td{\code{\verb~|casesOn~}/matcher/\code{\verb~|brecOn~} on \code{\verb~|List~}, \code{\verb~|Bool~}, \code{\verb~|Decidable~}; 68 k Nat \code{\verb~|beq~}; derived rules worth 1.9x}}
\tr{\td{depth and literals}\td{\code{\verb~|work/arena-site/tests/bad/perf/unfold-before-literal-bad.ndjson~}}\td{65 kB}\td{59}\td{5.3 G, 1.4 s}\td{reduction 99.6\% (measured on the \code{\verb~|-fallback~} variant)}\td{one \code{\verb~|Nat.rec~} loop on a literal, 2^19 deep, 524 k native adds; expected verdict: reject}}
}

\p{Cedar's own declarations as a whole are 47 / 30 / 17\% inference / conversion / reduction (section 3.2); \code{\verb~|cedar-hot-ax~} is 47 / 35 / 13\% of its checking instructions, in 3.4 s instead of 30 s and 133 MB instead of 905 MB; the full-cone slice has cedar's heat shape (\code{\verb~|BODY~}, rules on: top 100 = 72\%, cedar 70\%). Not recommended: \code{\verb~|init.ndjson~} (38\% reduction, more than Cedar's own declarations), con-leche (one declaration is 10\% of it), cslib (94.6\% of it is Mathlib).}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What this changes in the design documents}

\ul{
\li{ISA §8.5 (promotion): the expectation for mathlib does not hold (section 5 (i)). Either T2 is scoped to closed evaluation, entered by a per-declaration signal such as "10^4 reduction steps in this declaration" (reached by 0.01-0.14\% of declarations: 2-18\% of a library corpus's instructions, 93\% of reachability's), or it is dropped from v1 without loss on any library corpus.}
\li{ISA §3.6 (K2 trigger at 32 executions): workable as it is (nano-lean derives 553 rules on cedar, 2,240 on mathlib with it); rules are not free: the \code{\verb~|unfold-before-literal~} family is 12\% slower with them.}
\li{ISA §3.1 ("about 80\% of all nodes are executed once"): 93-95\% of nodes are private to one declaration, and infer misses per node are about 1 (cedar: 13.7 M misses for 14.7 M nodes).}
\li{A prepared hot set: because 82-91\% of cedar's and con-leche's \code{\verb~|BODY~} executions fall on the 300-1000 bodies hottest on Init and 94-96\% of their \code{\verb~|RULE~} executions on Init's 100 hottest rules, elaboration (and K2 derivation) of those objects can be done eagerly when their declarations are admitted, without heat counters, for every corpus that contains Init - which is all of them. Mathlib-based corpora need the same for Mathlib's structure projections, which a generic rule for structure projection functions covers.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Reproduction and raw data}

\p{Scripts (\code{\verb~|bench/workloads/~}): \code{\verb~|static-shape.rs~} + \code{\verb~|static-report.py~} (section 2); \code{\verb~|build-nano-lean-instrumented.sh~}, \code{\verb~|nl-run.sh VARIANT NAME~} (\code{\verb~|di~} | \code{\verb~|stats~} | \code{\verb~|nod~}), \code{\verb~|nl-perf-tests.sh~}, \code{\verb~|nl-perf-record.sh~} + \code{\verb~|perf-phases.py~} (phases), \code{\verb~|dyn-report.py~} (sections 3-4), \code{\verb~|origin-split.py~} (3.2), \code{\verb~|portable-hot-set.py~} (3.4), \code{\verb~|rpylean-stats.sh~}, \code{\verb~|build-gruszka-knobs.sh~}, \code{\verb~|gruszka-stats.sh~}, \code{\verb~|cross-report.py~} (3.7), \code{\verb~|slice-export.py~} (section 6). All third-party code ran in \src{bench/arena-docker.sh} containers pinned to CPUs 0-4 and 10-12 (one PMU, outside \code{\verb~|--arena-limits~}). Raw data (\code{\verb~|work/workloads/~}):}

\table{
\tr{\th{path}\th{content}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|static/<corpus>/{heat.txt,consts.tsv,segs.tsv,summary.txt}~}}\td{HEAT lines in the spike's format; per-constant kind, flags, static heat; per-declaration nodes and private nodes}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|dyn/nl/<corpus>.di.tsv~}; \code{\verb~|.stats.tsv~}, \code{\verb~|.nod.tsv~}}\td{per declaration: instructions and ns (uninstrumented checking code); 58 counters and the hottest unfolded constants and recursors (derived rules on / off)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|dyn/nl/<corpus>.dump.tsv~}, \code{\verb~|.nod.dump.tsv~}}\td{per constant: \code{\verb~|C~} delta (whnf, lazy, closed), firings, telescope executions; \code{\verb~|I~} iota per rule; \code{\verb~|PW~}/\code{\verb~|PE~} projections; \code{\verb~|N~} Nat ops by size; \code{\verb~|S~} string expansions; \code{\verb~|W~} whnf chain histogram}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|dyn/nl-perf/~}, \code{\verb~|dyn/nl-slices/~}}\td{the same for 17 \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests and the three slices}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|dyn/rpylean/<corpus>.{stats.txt,decls.jsonl}~}, \code{\verb~|dyn/gruszka/<corpus>.di.tsv~}}\td{the leaders' own statistics}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|perf/<name>.phases.txt~}, \code{\verb~|.folded.gz~}}\td{phase and self-instruction split, collapsed stacks (\code{\verb~|project-*~} = own declarations only, \code{\verb~|perf-*~} = perf tests, \code{\verb~|slice-*~} = slices)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|tables/~}, \code{\verb~|slices/~}}\td{\code{\verb~|static.md~}, \code{\verb~|dyn-summary.md~}, \code{\verb~|origin.md~}, \code{\verb~|portable.md~}, \code{\verb~|cross.md~}, \code{\verb~|top50-<corpus>.md~}, \code{\verb~|heat-<corpus>.md~}, \code{\verb~|perf/~}, \code{\verb~|slices/~}; name lists and the sliced exports of section 6}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|build/nano-lean-instrumented/~} (+ \code{\verb~|.patch~}), \code{\verb~|build/gruszka-knobs/~}}\td{the instrumented copy and its diff against v0.4.2 (\code{\verb~|src/wl.rs~} and the \code{\verb~|wl!~} call sites, 10 files), and the unmodified gruszka tree built with \code{\verb~|--features knobs~}}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Not measured, and limits}

\ul{
\li{The official kernel's own unfold counts (it cannot be instrumented); sokonanoda and pestka were not profiled; lengths of list literals (cons chains in the export) were not measured, only \code{\verb~|List.rec~} steps.}
\li{Instructions were attributed to phases by sampling and to declarations exactly, \strong{not} to individual constants: "cost per execution" figures are phase averages \verb~|[~est\verb~|]~.}
\li{nano-lean's \code{\verb~|-j 1~} session sees every declaration; with the arena's 4 workers each worker's counts are about a quarter, which makes every threshold of section 3.4 harder to reach, not easier.}
\li{rpylean's CEK entries are inferred from its counters, not counted; mathlib's top-50 list was inspected by hand only for the first 16 entries; roles are classified by name (\code{\verb~|*.casesOn~}, \code{\verb~|*.match_N~}, \code{\verb~|*.brecOn~}, \code{\verb~|inst*~}, \code{\verb~|_f~}) and by the shape \code{\verb~|fun .. self => self.i~}, so unusual names fall under "other definitions".}
}
}
